  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nConfidence in the lack of measurable precipitation through \nSaturday remains high as surface high pressure will remain in \ncontrol.\n\nA cold front is still forecast to drop across the region\ntonight. Moisture and support for lift still appear to be too \nmeager to support measurable precipitation. However, enough\nlow-level moisture is progged to create near-surface saturation\nas a subsidence inversion sets up behind the boundary late\ntonight. Areas of fog and low stratus may develop as a result.\nSome spotty non-measurable precipitation in the form of drizzle\ncannot be totally ruled out.\n\nThe boundary then eventually lifts back northward as a warm\nfront during the midweek period, opening the door to southwest\nflow aloft and a slow warming trend. By Thursday, high\ntemperatures some 10 to 15 degrees above normal appear likely.\nIt also appears likely that additional frost/freeze headlines \nwill not be needed ahead of the November 1st cutoff of the \ngrowing season. A shortwave riding through WSW/ENE oriented\n500mb contours across the Great Lakes later this week will most\nlikely not produce precipitation in our CWA, although a few \nmodel scenarios paint very light precipitation north of I-80\nThursday night.\n\nEnsembles are still suggesting that a southern Great Plains\nshortwave riding up the Ohio Valley could provide our next best\nchance of rain later this weekend While it does appear that this\nsystem may end the dry stretch for most of the area, strength \nand timing differences in the various members continues to cast\ndoubt on receiving a significant, wetting rainfall.",
